  "attuneMaskCredit": "Vivaldi, The Four Seasons, Op. 8. The Modena Chamber Orchestra. Public-domain recordings via Musopen.",
  "attuneFocus": "Slow shape",
  "physicsOpen": "The physics",
  "physicsTitle": "How the sound works",
  "physicsLead": "Two tones, and what they do not do.",
  "physicsHearingTitle": "The hearing barrier",
  "physicsHearingBody": "You cannot hear a binaural beat as its own note in the room. One steady tone goes to the left ear. A second tone, a few hertz away, goes to the right ear. Hearing builds the pulse from that split, and only then.\n\nStereo headphones keep the tones apart. Speakers, or one earbud, let both tones into both ears. The barrier fails, and there is no binaural beat.",
  "physicsCarrierTitle": "Carrier and beat",
  "physicsCarrierBody": "The carrier is the pitch you hear. In this app it runs from 100 to 400 Hz. The beat is the difference between the ears, not a second sound added on top.\n\nThe band names that difference. Delta is about 2 Hz, theta about 6, alpha about 10, and beta about 20. Those are pulse rates. They are not a promise that you will enter a named brain state.",
  "physicsFollowingTitle": "Frequency following response",
  "physicsFollowingBody": "Frequency following response is a research name for brain activity that sometimes tracks a repeating sound. The evidence is mixed. It is clearer for some clicks and loudness pulses than for a quiet binaural beat.\n\nThis app does not read your brain. It does not measure a response, and it does not place you in a state. If the sound feels like too much, stop.",
  "physicsLampTitle": "Lamp and laser",
  "physicsLampBody": "A lamp throws light in many directions. A laser holds one wavelength in step. Older writing used that contrast as a picture of scattered attention versus a narrow one.\n\nThe picture is not a measurement. MonkeyThrive does not turn the tone into a laser, and it does not steer the nervous system. Isochronic tones are different again: one tone, pulsed, which can feel sharper and does not need the two-ear split.",
  "physicsWarningKicker": "WARNING",
  "physicsWarning": "Do not drive or operate machinery while listening. The sound can make you drowsy or unsteady. It does not put you to sleep, and it does not override your nervous system. Stop if you feel dizzy, tense, or unwell. If you have a history of seizures, skip this tool.",
